      "provenance": {
        "notes": "NAME CLASH: New Guinea impatiens (Impatiens hawkeri) is a different plant, and NC State warns it is also mistaken for Catharanthus. Watering: NC State says moist, well-drained, low drought tolerance, wilts in hot dry spells, rots if overwatered; RHS moist but well-drained. Light: NC State part to full shade (scorches in too much sun), RHS partial shade recommended but full sun if kept well watered; stored bright_indirect (agent mapping). Size: NC State 0.5 to 2 ft both ways, RHS 0.1 to 0.5 m (15 to 60 cm in the text); NC State upper figure stored. Hardiness is tropical (NC State zones 10a to 11b, RHS H1B) so it is bedding or a pot plant; no indoor temperature range. Toxicity: only NC State's tags (non-toxic for cats and dogs) say anything, no agent or symptoms; the ASPCA page returned 404 and RHS is silent. One tag-only source: review. No human lines stored."
      },
